{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789717149,"nonce":"p17uRXoIxzfMj5LUTtatbeSHyNk=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABR6WrGA","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/138","origin":"github"},"title":"Consider porting enclosure radiation from jno.Coupling to jno.derived","message":"## Context\n\n`jno.derived` (added on `feat/droplet-features`) is the value-side spelling of nonlocal coupling: a pure-JAX rule on the state produces a **nodal field** usable anywhere a field is, lagged out of the tangent. `jno.Coupling` remains the residual-side spelling, with the nonlocality carried **exactly** in the tangent.\n\nEnclosure radiation fits both. `docs/fem/geometry.md` now documents the derived spelling alongside the `Coupling` one, and `tests/test_fem_enclosure_radiation.py::test_radiosity_as_a_derived_field_matches_the_coupling_and_the_analytic_series` covers it. **The default was deliberately left unchanged** — this issue is the record of why a full port is attractive and what would have to be checked first.\n\n## The case for porting\n\nMeasured on the concentric-cylinder enclosure (m = 512 enclosure elements, 13 083 DOFs, CPU, float64):\n\n| | residual | JVP (one Krylov matvec) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `jno.Coupling` | 429 ms | **289 ms** |\n| `jno.derived`  | 418 ms | **8.5 ms** |\n\n**34x on the tangent, nothing on the residual** — exactly the predicted shape. Under `Coupling` the dense `(I - rho F)` radiosity solve is differentiated through, so it runs in *every* Krylov iteration and the tangent couples every enclosure element to every other; under `derived` it is `stop_gradient`ed out and the tangent is purely local and sparse. The dense solve still runs once per residual either way.\n\nSecondary benefit: the derived form has an **assembled** sparse Jacobian, so `fem.solve(nonlinear=jno.solve.newton(direct=True))` works. The `Coupling` path is matrix-free only.\n\nAccuracy: the derived spelling reproduces the analytic concentric-cylinder series to 0.05%, and agrees with the `Coupling` solve to **0.001 K** on a 300-1000 K problem. The only formal difference is one P1 boundary-mass smoothing of the flux distribution (total radiative power is conserved *exactly*, since `sum_i (M_bnd)_ij = int phi_j ds`), consistent at O(h^2).\n\n## Why it was not done now\n\nLagging trades a Newton carrying the full `T^4` radiative coupling in its tangent for successive substitution on it. Convergence becomes linear at a rate set by the radiative-to-conductive stiffness ratio `~4 eps sigma T^3 L / k`, and for a **radiation-dominated** enclosure it can stall or diverge. The concentric-cylinder case is conduction-dominated, so it converges easily and tells us nothing about the hard regime. The remedy exists (`jno.solve.picard(damping=...)`, `newton(line_search=True)`) and the failure is loud, but a silent field regression is still a real risk: someone's furnace stops converging and the test suite says nothing.\n\n`tests/test_fem_enclosure_radiation.py:758`'s `|grad - FD| \u003c 3e-2` assertion also **cannot** simply be re-pointed at the derived path — the Picard adjoint differs from the true adjoint by the radiative coupling block.\n\n## What a port would need\n\n- [ ] A radiation-dominated benchmark (high `eps sigma T^3 L / k`) exercising the regime the current tests do not, with the damping needed to converge it recorded.\n- [ ] A measured gradient tolerance for the derived path, stated as the Picard adjoint rather than silently loosened.\n- [ ] A decision on the default: port outright, or keep both and change only which one the docs lead with.\n- [ ] Timings at a few `m` (the 34x above is one point; the gap should grow with `m`).","files":null}]}